EXT. FROZEN SEA - STRANDED WHALING SHIP - DAY

The hull of a three-masted whaler locked in pack ice. Rigging furred with rime. A gale is dying. The light is low and silver.

On the listing deck, ANNIKA LUND, 30s, wrapped in oilskins, tends a tin stove. OLEG VARGA, 50s, harpooner's shoulders, gaunt, feeds splinters into the flame. DR. PELL, 60s, round spectacles, shivers under a sealskin blanket and counts tins of beans.

A figure appears on the ice, dragging a sled. He stops at the rail. Tall, bearded, hollow-cheeked, frost in his beard.

STRANGER
Is there room by the fire?

ANNIKA
(rifle half-lifted)
Who are you?

STRANGER
A man who walked a long way.

OLEG
No one walks on this ice.

STRANGER
I did.

DR. PELL
Annika, let him up. He's half dead.

Annika lowers the rifle. Oleg throws down a rope. The stranger climbs, crumples on the deck. Annika drapes a coat over him.

ANNIKA
Do you have a name?

STRANGER
Hollis. Matthew Hollis. Third mate of the Albatross.

DR. PELL
The Albatross went down in March.

HOLLIS
Four of us got to the ice. I'm what's left.

OLEG
Four? Where are the others?

Hollis stares into the stove. Does not answer.

DR. PELL
Beans, Mr. Hollis?

HOLLIS
Please.

Pell hands him a tin and a spoon. Hollis eats slowly, like a man learning how.

ANNIKA
Oleg. Make room.

Oleg shifts over without a word. Hollis settles against the mast, eyes half shut, the fire on his face.

OLEG
How far is the coast?

HOLLIS
Two weeks. If the ice holds.

DR. PELL
And if it doesn't?

HOLLIS
Then you've already had your supper.

Annika almost laughs. Oleg looks away. The stove ticks.
